  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Temperatures will remain seasonable and slightly \nabove average, with considerable mid and high-level clouds and \npatchy light rain/sprinkles over the Piedmont, through Mon. \n\nA shortwave perturbation, evident in regional radar and GOES-E \nsatellite data over the TN Valley and embedded within an elongated \nsynoptic trough from the upr Great Lakes to the ern Gulf, will \nprogress ewd and across and offshore the Middle Atlantic and \nCarolinas through Mon. It will be accompanied by considerable mid \nand high-level cloudiness and virga. Some of the precipitation may \nreach the surface in the form of patchy, very light measurable rain \nof a hundredth or two, or non-measurable sprinkles. Those occurrences \nwill be favored along the track of the shortwave disturbance and \nstrongest ascent from s-cntl VA to n-cntl NC Mon morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Patchy dense fog will be possible Tue and Wed \nmorning, especially over the ne Piedmont and nrn-cntl Coastal Plain.\n\nSkies will clear from west to east with the passage of the \naforementioned shortwave perturbation late Mon-Mon early night, then \nremain generally so until deep moisture increases ahead of the next \ntrough aloft late Wed and Wed night. Related mainly clear, and calm \nto light nely to ely stirring within the lingering presence of a \nsurface ridge axis, will favor good radiational cooling both Mon and \nTue night. As the related airmass gradually modifies and low-levels \nmoisten through that time, the risk of morning fog will increase. \nSome may become dense over the ne Piedmont and nrn and cntl Coastal \nPlain, where the greatest overlap of calm and moisture recovery is \nanticipated.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Chance of showers from late Wednesday night through \nFriday night or early Saturday. Above-normal temperatures expected \nthrough the week.\n\nA frontal system approaching the Mississippi Valley Wednesday \nmorning will lift a warm front north across the Mid-Atlantic on \nThursday ahead of an approaching cold front. The primary boundary \nwill cross the region Thursday into Friday before pushing offshore \nFriday evening, followed by a weak, dry reinforcing boundary early \nSaturday. \n\nShowers are expected to develop over the western Piedmont late \nWednesday night before spreading eastward Thursday and Thursday \nnight. Behind the initial boundary, drier air will begin filtering \ninto the region Friday night. Model guidance has trended drier over \nrecent runs, cutting back both PoPs and QPF; consequently, \nconfidence in widespread precipitation remains low. For now, the \nhighest PoPs are focused Thursday afternoon, where enough \ninstability may develop to support a few isolated rumbles of thunder.\n\nTemperatures will run 10 to 14 degrees above climatological normal \nthroughout the period. Highs will consistently reach the mid 70s to \nlower 80s, with mild overnight lows generally in the mid 50s to \nlower 60s.",
